One common way improper use of Java APIs leads to vulnerabilities is through **uncontrolled deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on untrusted data from a network request, an attacker can provide a specially crafted serialized object that executes arbitrary code during the deserialization process.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.URL;

public class VulnerableApp {
    // This method is vulnerable to insecure deserialization attacks
    public void processRequest(InputStream inputStream) throws Exception {
        // The code takes an untrusted stream and converts it directly into an object
        // An attacker can pass a malicious gadget chain here to achieve RCE
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
        System.out.println("Processed object: " + obj.toString());
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.HashSet;

public class SecureApp {
    // To fix this, we use a whitelist approach (Look-ahead Deserialization)
    // to ensure only expected classes are instantiated.
    public void processRequest(InputStream inputStream) throws Exception {
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream) {
            @Override
            public void resolveClass(ObjectStreamClass desc) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
                // Only allow specific, safe classes to be deserialized
                Set<String> allowedClasses = new HashSet<>();
                allowedClasses.add("java.lang.String");
                allowedClasses.add("com.myapp.dto.UserDTO");

                if (!allowedClasses.contains(desc.getName())) {
                    throw new InvalidClassException("Unauthorized deserialization attempt: " + desc.getName());
                }
                super.resolveClass(desc);
            }
        }) {
            Object obj = ois.readObject();
            System.out.println("Processed object: " + obj.toString());
        }
    }
}
```

